    <summary>Aligns a macro series shot at stepped focus distances and merges it into one image with full depth of field, offline focus stacking software with RAW input and 16-bit TIFF output.</summary>
    <description>This package installs Focus Stacking Software for Macro Photography, focus stacking software for 64-bit Windows that builds one sharp image from frames shot at different focus distances. Load 20 to 100 frames of a stack, and the program lines them up, picks the crispest detail at each point and writes the composite on the local machine. Nothing leaves the PC.

![Focus Stacking Software screenshot 1](https://download.softorbits.com/files/pkg-img/fst/fst-v1-series.png)

## How to use Focus Stacking Software for Macro Photography

1. Run the program after Chocolatey has installed it.
2. Use Open series for the frames of one stack, or choose to treat each subfolder as a series.
3. Leave Align frames on for a tripod or rail sequence.
4. Select the Detail or Smooth merge method, then tune Radius and Smoothing if needed.
5. Press Stack. The stage line reports progress, and Stop cancels the run.
6. Add Background blur if the backdrop looks too busy.
7. Click Save result and pick 16-bit TIFF, JPEG or PNG.

Trial note: an unregistered copy aligns and merges the series and previews the finished picture, but it cannot save, and the subfolder mode computes only the first series. Registering enables saving.

![Focus Stacking Software screenshot 2](https://download.softorbits.com/files/pkg-img/fst/fst-v1-result.png)

## Focus stacking with two merge methods

Detail compares wavelet coefficients and keeps the sharpest source pixel, which suits textured subjects. Smooth estimates a depth map and blends along it, which helps with gradients and plain backgrounds.

## What the focus stacking software reads and writes

- RAW from CR2, CR3, NEF, ARW, DNG, ORF and RAF cameras through LibRaw, so no TIFF conversion comes first.
- JPEG and TIFF frames.
- Results as 16-bit TIFF for editing, or JPEG and PNG for a listing.

Memory use grows with resolution only. A stack of 100 frames costs the same RAM as 20 frames from the same camera, about 200 MB per megapixel.

![Focus Stacking Software screenshot 3](https://download.softorbits.com/files/pkg-img/fst/fst-v1-blur.png)

## Background blur after the merge

The merge already knows how far each pixel sits, so a slider can push the backdrop out of focus while the subject keeps its original pixels. Lens gives round bokeh highlights and Soft gives a plain blur.

## Requirements and limits

Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit, and 8 GB of RAM for 24 to 45 megapixel frames. There is no 32-bit build. Handheld sequences are out of scope, because alignment corrects scale and shift, not parallax.

## Who it is for

- Numismatists and coin dealers photographing lots for grading or auction.
- Museums and entomology collections digitizing specimens.
- Studios that focus stack photos of small products on several workstations set up with Chocolatey.

## Trial and license

The package installs the free trial of a commercial program, and full use needs a license. A lifetime, 1-year, monthly or business license unlocks saving. Learn more about [focus stacking for macro photos](https://www.softorbits.net/focus-stacking-software/?utm_source=chocolatey&amp;utm_medium=package).

## FAQ

### What is the point of focus stacking software?

It gives a close-up photo more depth of field than one exposure can hold. The program merges frames focused at different depths into a single sharp image.

### How much does focus stacking software cost?

The price depends on the license you pick: lifetime, 1-year, monthly or business. Trying it on your own frames is free.

### Is focus stacking worth the extra shots?

For macro subjects it usually is, since at high magnification a single frame keeps only a thin slice in focus. The stack recovers the rest of the subject.
